  are in one file, so this is a bounded change.

  The snag: nothing in the app has a user ID. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m collects only username and password, and the stub's own return
  value ([38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m) is the closest thing to an identity — and it only exists [3mafter[0m login. So
  "track who logged in" doesn't have an obvious value to pass in, and the options differ in what you end up with:

  - [1mClient tracking ID[0m — generate a UUID once, persist in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, pass as [38;5;153muserId[39m. Gives you a stable handle for
    correlating login attempts from the same browser, including failed ones. It is [3mnot[0m an authenticated identity —
    anyone can clear or forge it. No UI change. Cheap to swap out later.
  - [1mNew form field[0m — add a [38;5;153muserId[39m input to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and read it in the handler. Gives a real user-supplied ID, but it
    duplicates username for the person filling the form, and it's self-asserted, so it's no more trustworthy than the
    tracking ID. Changes the UI, so it's the most visible to undo.
  - [1mDon't add a parameter[0m — have [38;5;153mlogin[39m derive and return the id in its result instead. Tracks who [3msuccessfully[0m logged
    in, which is what the server would authoritatively know anyway. But it doesn't match your request, and it can't
    attribute failed attempts.

  My recommendation is the client tracking ID: it matches your request literally (a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter on [38;5;153mlogin[39m), needs no
  UI change, and covers failed attempts, which is usually the point of login tracking. The thing it can't do is prove
  identity — if that's what you need, that has to come from the server response, not a parameter.

  One more thing worth flagging either way: [38;5;153mlogin[39m and the handler both [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m already, so whatever you pass will
  land in the browser console alongside the username.
